<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of Holland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1806">1806</a>–<a href="/years/1810">1810</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of Holland was a monarchy created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1806 to replace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a>, with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-bonaparte">Louis Bonaparte</a> as king. Louis tried to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> interests, enforcing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental System</a> only loosely, and he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herman-willem-daendels">Herman Willem Daendels</a> to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. Displeased, Napoleon forced Louis to abdicate in 1810 and annexed the kingdom to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> adopted a more moderate constitution in October 1801, and a new one drafted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rutger-jan-schimmelpenninck">Rutger Jan Schimmelpenninck</a> in March 1805 restored the unitary state, with Schimmelpenninck as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-pensionary">Grand Pensionary</a>. Napoleon Bonaparte remained troubled by the instability of the regime. He thought only a monarchy could keep the country from succumbing to British pressure or longing for the old regime, and he found that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> did not meet their military duties and that Schimmelpenninck was not a docile ally. By 1806 the Swiss Confederation and the Batavian Republic were the last remaining sister republics, while Napoleon had already made kingdoms for his family elsewhere.</p><p>Schimmelpenninck objected that a hereditary head of state did not suit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> national character. On 28 April 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> gave Dutch diplomats eight days to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-bonaparte">Louis Bonaparte</a> as king. The members of the government ratified a treaty without consulting the people. Louis entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> on 23 June 1806, was never crowned, and had problems with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> throughout his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> intended Louis to be little more than a French prefect, but Louis tried to act independently. He learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, adopted the spelling Lodewijk and declared himself Dutch. He centralised the state, appointing the mayors of the larger cities, and introduced a countrywide tax system, a Civil Registry and Land Registry, and in 1809 a penal code and civil code based on French models that respected Dutch customs. He promoted equality for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Catholics and dissenters, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-netherlands-academy-of-arts-and-sciences">Royal Netherlands Academy of Arts and Sciences</a> and the Royal Museum were founded in his reign. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> gunpowder disaster of 1807 he started a national collection, gave a large sum and opened Huis ten Bosch as a hospital.</p><p>In 1807 the kingdom gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jever">Jever</a>, and was divided into eleven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>. After the Walcheren Campaign in 1809 it had to give up all territory south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Louis quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> over three matters. He refused to tier the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">national debt</a>, which would have hurt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> investors; he refused conscription, ordering only Dutch orphans to enlist, which provoked a riot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a> on 14 July 1809; and he enforced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental System</a> weakly, since a strict policy would damage Dutch maritime trade. After the Walcheren Campaign Napoleon called him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and annexed the territories between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>.</p><p>Louis abdicated on 1 July 1810 in favour of his son, French troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> on 4 July, and an imperial decree abolished the kingdom and incorporated it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>, which held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> until 1813. It was the first formal monarchy on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> territory since 1581.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kingdom of Holland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Herman Willem Daendels, appointed governor-general by Louis Bonaparte’s <b>Kingdom of Holland</b>, arrived in 1808, centralised the administration of Java, curbed the Javanese courts and built the Great Post Road along the north coast with forced labour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom itself had been created in 1815, when the Congress of Vienna united the former Dutch Republic, which the French had turned into the Batavian Republic and then the <b>Kingdom of Holland</b>, with the former Austrian Netherlands under William I of the House of Orange-Nassau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
